course: dictionary
lesson: 3173
title: Who is an employer?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An employer is a person or company that pays people to work for them. The person doing the job is the employee, or worker. «My employer pays me every month for the work I do» shows the money going from the employer to the worker.
explain: An employer can be big, like a company that hires thousands of people, or small, like one shop owner with one helper. Look at the words around employer to find out. Words like pays, hire and wages are clues that someone is an employer.
explain: «A job interview is a meeting where an employer asks questions to decide whom to hire.» Here the clues are interview, questions and hire. They tell you the employer is the one choosing the worker, not the one looking for a job.
fact: An employer is a person or company that pays people to work for them.
fact: The worker who is paid by an employer is called an employee.
fact: In money lessons, employers pay wages, and workers may sign contracts and get holiday pay.
fact: A «reference» is a short message from a past employer saying a person was reliable.
fact: «Sacked» or «fired» means losing a job because of something the person did, and employers usually give warnings first.
exercise: What is an employer? | *A person or company that pays people to work for them | A meeting where people ask questions | A shop that sells things to customers | A worker who is paid to do a job
exercise: «My employer pays me every Friday.» In this sentence, who is the employee? | The bank that holds the money | *The worker who is paid to do the job | The company that pays | The shop where the job is done
exercise: «A job interview is a meeting where an employer asks questions to decide whom to hire.» Which word means choosing someone for a job? | *hire | meeting | holiday | questions
test: «On a zero-hours contract the employer promises no set number of hours.» What does this mean for the worker? | The worker never gets paid | The hours are fixed for a whole year | *The hours and pay can change every week | The employer must do the hours instead
test: «Probation at work is a trial period, and it has nothing to do with crime.» Does probation mean a punishment? | Yes, it is a punishment for breaking rules | Yes, it means being sacked at once | Yes, it means going to prison | *No, it is a trial period when the worker and employer see if the job fits
exam: Which is the best meaning of employer? | A child who helps at home for pocket money | A worker who does the job for pay | *A person or company that pays people to work for them | A meeting where people ask questions
